1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,202
Modules draw boundaries. Collections fill the day-to-day work.

2
00:00:04,202 --> 00:00:08,403
Almost every Java program needs an ordered sequence of values.

3
00:00:08,403 --> 00:00:12,605
That is the List interface — indexed, ordered, and everywhere.

4
00:00:12,605 --> 00:00:16,129
ArrayList. LinkedList. When each one earns its keep.

5
00:00:16,129 --> 00:00:19,246
Today we choose lists with intent — not habit.

6
00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:24,058
Order with random access — or order with cheap inserts. Know the trade.

7
00:00:24,058 --> 00:00:25,710
Episode Twenty-One.

8
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:29,276
Lists — ordered collections in java.util.

9
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:31,493
List is a contract in java.util.

10
00:00:31,493 --> 00:00:34,611
Elements have positions — zero-based indexes.

11
00:00:34,611 --> 00:00:39,877
Duplicates are allowed. Null may be allowed depending on the implementation.

12
00:00:39,877 --> 00:00:44,519
add, get, set, remove, size, contains — the verbs you already know.

13
00:00:44,519 --> 00:00:50,269
Prefer coding to List, not to a concrete class — until performance forces a choice.

14
00:00:50,269 --> 00:00:52,902
The interface keeps your API flexible.

15
00:00:52,902 --> 00:00:55,166
ArrayList is the default workhorse.

16
00:00:55,166 --> 00:00:59,758
Backed by a resizable array. Amortized constant-time append at the end.

17
00:00:59,758 --> 00:01:04,350
Random access by index is fast — get and set are essentially array ops.

18
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:09,136
Inserts and removes in the middle shift elements — that can get expensive.

19
00:01:09,136 --> 00:01:12,758
Give an initial capacity when you know the size roughly.

20
00:01:12,758 --> 00:01:15,280
For most application lists, start here.

21
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:17,776
LinkedList is a doubly linked structure.

22
00:01:17,776 --> 00:01:22,268
Cheap inserts and removes when you already hold the right node position.

23
00:01:22,268 --> 00:01:26,697
Random access by index walks the chain — do not pretend it is an array.

24
00:01:26,697 --> 00:01:32,250
It also implements Queue and Deque — useful as a deque more than as a random-access list.

25
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:36,305
If you mainly get by index, LinkedList is usually the wrong tool.

26
00:01:36,305 --> 00:01:39,986
Measure the access pattern before you romanticize pointers.

27
00:01:39,986 --> 00:01:40,884
How to choose.

28
00:01:40,884 --> 00:01:44,218
Most reads by index, appends at the end — ArrayList.

29
00:01:44,218 --> 00:01:50,054
Heavy middle inserts with sequential traversal — consider LinkedList, or rethink the model.

30
00:01:50,054 --> 00:01:54,671
Often a better answer is a different structure — Queue, Deque, or a map.

31
00:01:54,671 --> 00:01:57,877
Do not pick LinkedList because it sounds advanced.

32
00:01:57,877 --> 00:02:01,404
Pick the structure that matches how you touch the data.

33
00:02:01,404 --> 00:02:03,918
List pitfalls that show up in reviews.

34
00:02:03,918 --> 00:02:09,739
Modifying a list while iterating with a for-each — ConcurrentModificationException risk.

35
00:02:09,739 --> 00:02:13,642
Use an Iterator remove, or collect changes and apply after.

36
00:02:13,642 --> 00:02:19,662
Arrays.asList returns a fixed-size list backed by an array — not a fully mutable ArrayList.

37
00:02:19,662 --> 00:02:25,285
List.of creates unmodifiable lists — great for constants, surprising if you call add.

38
00:02:25,285 --> 00:02:28,990
Know whether your list is growable before you mutate it.

39
00:02:28,990 --> 00:02:30,413
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:30,413 --> 00:02:35,069
One — LinkedList everywhere for inserts that never happen in the middle.

41
00:02:35,069 --> 00:02:39,337
Two — raw types — List without generics — type safety thrown away.

42
00:02:39,337 --> 00:02:45,092
Three — exposing a mutable ArrayList from an API that should return an unmodifiable view.

43
00:02:45,092 --> 00:02:49,037
Also — ignoring equals contract when lists hold custom types.

44
00:02:49,037 --> 00:02:52,400
Lists are simple. Careless APIs make them expensive.

45
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,872
Interview question — ArrayList versus LinkedList?

46
00:02:55,872 --> 00:03:00,619
ArrayList — array-backed, fast index access, costly middle inserts.

47
00:03:00,619 --> 00:03:05,863
LinkedList — node-backed, weak random access, better as a deque sometimes.

48
00:03:05,863 --> 00:03:09,406
Default choice in apps is almost always ArrayList.

49
00:03:09,406 --> 00:03:13,657
Mention Big-O briefly, then talk about real access patterns.

50
00:03:13,657 --> 00:03:17,058
That answer is practical — not textbook theater.

51
00:03:17,058 --> 00:03:21,836
Ordered sequences are clear. Next — uniqueness and hashing.

52
00:03:21,836 --> 00:03:23,942
Episode Twenty-Two — Sets.

53
00:03:23,942 --> 00:03:28,558
HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet — and when order matters.

54
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:29,692
See you there.
